  "content": {
    "id": "p37-q4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "Show that $f(x)=2x^3-5$ has a horizontal point of inflection and find its coordinates.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Horizontal point of inflection at $(0,-5)$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f'(x)&=6x^2\\\\6x^2&=0\\\\x&=0\\\\f(0)&=2(0)^3-5=-5\\\\f''(x)&=12x\\\\f''(0)&=0\\\\f''(-1)&=-12,\\qquad f''(1)=12\\end{align*}$$\nSince $f''(x)=12x$ is negative for $x<0$ and positive for $x>0$, the concavity changes from down to up at $x=0$. Also, $f'(0)=0$, so $(0,-5)$ is a horizontal point of inflection.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
